    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f1e5551_da4f2d7f",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003665
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-26T23:35:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is the one of the bugs I alluded to in our emails when this code was part of cpufreq_stats.c. A cluster (as in, \"policy\". Not ARM\u0027s unintuitive definition of a cluster) can go offline/online multiple times. You are going to keep leaking memory because you never subtract next_offset when a cluster goes offline.\n\nI think a simpler solution is to not try to follow policy creation and destruction. Instead, just keep the \"times\" struct in a local per-cpu array (all CPUs in a policy will point to the same \"times\" struct). You\u0027ll populated the time_in_state for CPUs the first time the policy is created and then leave them untouched. That\u0027ll keep the code a lot simpler at the expense of \"wasting\" a few KB when a cluster is offline.\n\nAnother benefit is, you won\u0027t lose stats every time a cluster goes offline (like cpufreq_stats does).\n\nTo be clear, none of this precludes you from having one array in the process struct to store all time in state details across all CPUs.",
